/T (Question 44. In Python, how does the list comprehension [x for x in iterable if x] filter the elements of the iterable? A. It includes only the elements that are numerically zero. B. It excludes any elements that evaluate to False in a boolean context. C. It doubles the elements that evaluate to True. D. It randomizes the order of elements based on their truth value. Correct Answer: B Explanation: The list comprehension [x for x in iterable if x] in Python filters out any elements that evaluate to False in a boolean context, such as 0, False, None, empty sequences, and other "falsy" values. This construct is an efficient way to remove unwanted elements based on their truthiness.)
