/T (Question 47. In what scenario would using a deque from the collections module be more advantageous than using a list for adding and removing elements from both ends? A. When you need to frequently access elements by index. B. When you want to minimize memory usage for small collections. C. When you require O\(1\) time complexity for appending and popping elements from both ends. D. When you need to maintain the order of elements strictly. Correct Answer: C Explanation: A deque \(double-ended queue\) allows for O\(1\) time complexity for appending and popping elements from both ends, making it highly efficient for scenarios requiring frequent insertions and deletions. In contrast, lists incur O\(n\) time complexity for these operations at the beginning of the list, which can lead to performance bottlenecks in such cases.)
